# DECISIONS — <project>

Append-only. Newest last. **Never edited, never reordered, never deleted** — a
decision that turned out wrong is superseded by a later entry that says so, and
both stay. The value of this file is that it records what was believed at the
time, which is exactly what an edit destroys.

One entry per decision that a later reader could otherwise not reconstruct from
the code. Not a changelog: if the diff explains it, it does not belong here.

Format, kept short enough that recording a decision is not a tax:

---

## D-001 — <one line: what was decided>

**Decided.** What is now true, in one or two sentences.

**Evidence.** What was measured, read or executed. Name the file, the command,
or the revision — a decision whose evidence is "it seemed better" is a
preference, and should say so rather than borrow the authority of a measurement.

**Reverses on.** The observation that would make this wrong. A decision with no
stated reversal condition cannot be revisited honestly later, because there is
nothing to check it against.

